      "passage": "Shadows stretched long into the void deck as Rose approached with hesitant steps. In just an hour, she and her family would leave the familiar behind, moving into a new neighbourhood and a life unknown. Lily, her bosom buddy, was waiting on the marble bench. Over the years, this had become their favourite haunt, their refuge and their place for sharing whispered secrets. Without a word, Rose sank down beside her. The dread of having to part ways soon weighed heavily on their hearts.\n\nJust beyond the bench was where Rose had first learnt to ride a bicycle when she was six. She could recall the soreness in her thighs as she pedalled, her father's steady voice urging her on and the sting of scraped knees after every fall. On a pivotal try, her father had held the back of her seat and promised not to let go. However, he lied. It was the only way Rose could discover her own balance. When she finally did, it opened the door to a new joy – weekend rides with Lily. Together, they laughed, raced and zigzagged between the pillars of the shared space like seasoned riders in their own private arena.\n\nMoments later, Rose and Lily walked on slowly. Near the lift lobby, Rose paused. \"Do you remember ... when we came here for your grandmother's funeral?\" she asked softly. Lily nodded. \"I was nine. You were the first to arrive. Your mum brought curry puffs for my family and your dad assisted in arranging the chairs.\" The evening had been quiet, except for the sound of muffled weeping from time to time. Many were laden with sadness. Yet, there were also moments of laughter, stories and a celebration of the blessed life Lily's grandmother had lived. Rose did not say much that day, but her presence had been Lily's anchor of support in a sea of grief.\n\nWhen they reached the area near the mailboxes, Rose and Lily smiled as they remembered a wedding from when they were eleven. Their neighbours, Ahmad and Nora, had tied the knot. That day, the void deck came alive with music and the smell of spicy, savoury dishes drifted all around them. Dressed in bright colours, the girls had danced ecstatically. With every twirl, joy bubbled up inside them. Best of all, the girls tasted every delectable dish twice. These memories from the wedding were a snapshot of childhood happiness that was ingrained in their minds.\n\nAs they passed the noticeboard, a recent memory surfaced. Just a month ago, Rose and Lily had volunteered at the neighbourhood food donation drive that had been held at that very space. For hours, they had gathered and sorted a variety of food items donated by friendly residents. Although the tasks were laborious, the sense of purpose – to help the food collected would eventually bless the less fortunate – had kept the girls going. Thinking about the difference they had made in the lives of the underprivileged, the girls beamed with pride.\n\n\"Rose, it's time to go,\" her mother called out gently. Hearing that, Lily took out a small sketchbook and handed it to Rose. Inside were drawings of the cherished moments they had shared – Rose's first bicycle, the kind face of Lily's grandmother, the wedding day feast and the donation booth. In the middle of the page was a drawing of a big red heart, a symbol of love the girls had for their shared memories. Touched by the meaningful gift, Rose teared. For Rose, the void deck was not just a space under their flats, but a place filled with laughter, friendship and memories from her childhood. She firmly believed that surroundings might change, but relationships would always remain. \"Bye, Lily. I'll surely be back to visit,\" Rose promised.",
